Organization = {Univ Porto, Sch Econ \& Management; Univ Minho, Sch Engn},
Abstract = {Energy efficiency has become a critical policy tool around the world,
   helping to meet the substantial growth in energy demand. With rising
   economic and population growth in the commercial sector, India needs
   extra 80\% floor space estimated for2030. Healthcare is one of the most
   influenced sectors, which is experiencing significant growth regarding
   increasing capacity of hospital buildings, to meet the needs of growing
   population of India. A growth rate of 12-15\% is seen in the last decade
   in India's health sector and expected to rise at a faster pace.
   This paper aims at the implementation methods, and challenges in the
   healthcare sector for achieving energy saving by energy efficiency
   measures. The techniques addressed in this study for energy-saving
   include energy consumption per gross floor area, standard hospital
   electrical stock (lighting, televisions, and other electrical
   appliances) replacement with maximizing energy efficient stocks, and
   building stock retrofitting for an old ageing building. The further part
   of the study also focuses on discussing the Government policy to the
   factors affecting decision making in achieving energy efficiency in
   urban/rural and public/private hospital.},
