Note = {8th International Multisensory Research Forum, Univ Sydney, Sydney,
   AUSTRALIA, JUL, 2007},
Abstract = {In temporal numerosity judgment, observers systematically underestimate
   the number of pulses. The strongest underestimations occur when stimuli
   are presented with a short interstimulus interval (ISI) and are stronger
   for vision than for audition and touch. We investigated if multisensory
   presentation leads to a reduction of underestimation. Participants were
   presented with 2 to 10 (combinations of) auditory beeps, tactile taps to
   the index finger and visual flashes at different ISIs (20 to 320 ms).
   For all presentation modes, we found underestimation, except for small
   number of pulses. A control experiment showed that the latter is due to
   a (cognitive) range effect. Averaged over conditions, the order of
   performance of sensory modalities is touch, audition and last vision.
   Generally, multisensory presentation improves performance over the
   unisensory presentations. For larger ISIs (160 and 320 ms), we found a
   tendency toward a reduction in variance for the multisensory
   presentation modes. For smaller ISIs (20 and 40 ms), we found a
   reduction in underestimation, but an increase in variance for the
   multisensory presentation modes. In the discussion, we relate these two
   findings to Maximum Likelihood Estimation (MLE) models predicting that
   multisensory integration reduces variance. (c) 2008 Elsevier B.V. All
   rights reserved.},
