   SWEDEN, JUN 23-27, 2013},
Organization = {Agora Biosystems; Sigtunastiftelsen; Vetenskapsradet; Springer; Uppsala
   Univ; Swedish Univ Agr Sci},
Abstract = {In this paper, a possible contribution of electroencephalogram (EEG) on
   spike transmission through multiple brain regions was evaluated by using
   computer simulations of a multi-layered network under the hypothesis
   that EEG and spikes are reciprocally activated. In the results, EEG
   activities across the multiple layers appeared to be globally
   synchronized, which was shown to produce a globally consistent pathway
   of spike transmission through the layers. This suggests that the EEG
   synchronization contributes to organize the activity transmission
   through multiple regions.},
