   Malarzewski, Lukasz and Owczarek, Piotr},
Editor = {Halada, L and Baca, A and Boltiziar, M},
Title = {LANDSCAPE DEGRADATION AND ITS EFFECT ON THE SOIL-VEGETATION RELATIONS
   WITHIN JUNIPER FOREST IN THE FANN MOUNTAINS (WESTERN PAMIR-ALAY)},
Booktitle = {LANDSCAPE AND LANDSCAPE ECOLOGY},
Year = {2016},
Pages = {168-175},
Note = {17th International Symposium on Landscape Ecology - Landscape and
   Landscape Ecology, Nitra, SLOVAKIA, MAY 27-29, 2015},
Organization = {Slovak Acad Sci, Inst Landscape Ecol},
Abstract = {uniper woodland landscapes in the Fann Mountains (Tajikistan, Central
   Asia) are degraded as a result of mass felling for economic use. This
   causes erosion and changes the chemistry of the soil environment, which
   leads to the landscape degradation. Particular attention is paid to the
   change of mountain juniper forests, which is a consequence of soil
   degradation, as well as depletion of floristic communities and their
   functioning. At higher and inaccessible parts of the study area
   landscape has a more natural character than in the areas available for
   human activity. Vegetation is represented by juniper forests and the
   main species are Juniperus semiglobosa Regel. and J. seravschanica Kom.
   The floristic composition of the two associations shows similarity in
   terms of the dominant and co-occurring species (Artemisia lehmanniana,
   A. dracunculus, Ligularia thomsonii, Poa litvinoviana, P. relaxa, Ferula
   kokanica, Hipericum scabrum, Ziziphora pamiroalaica, Gentiana oliveri,
   Campanula glomerata, Scabiosa soongorica, Festuca sulcata, Cotoneaster
   oliganthus, Rosa fedtschenkoana, R. ecae, Ephedra equisetina, Astragalus
   variegatus, Spirea hypericifolia, Lonicera bracteolaris, L. simulatrix,
   Berberis oblongata).
   The analysed soil shows varied granulometric composition. At the highest
   altitudes gravel-stony fraction strongly predominates, while at lower
   altitudes relatively medium and earthy fractions predominate in the
   topsoil layer. Soil pH in all the investigated samples is neutral, which
   is connected with parent rocks. The available P, Mg, K are dominant in
   the upper horizons. The organic carbon (OC) content is clearly dependent
   on the amount of Nt (total nitrogen) and vice versa. The largest amounts
   of OC (10-25 g.kg(-1)) and Nt (1.2-2.0 g.kg(-1)) occur in the upper soil
   horizons.},
