   International-Association-for-Engineering-Geology-and-the-Environment,
   VANCOUVER, CANADA, SEP 21-25, 1998},
Organization = {Int Assoc Engn Geol \& Environm},
Abstract = {Construction of urban shallow shielded tunnels is usually associated
   with some surface and subsurface subsidence. The predicted magnitude and
   spreading of these movements can be utilized to identify the possible
   damages to existing near-by buildings and other structures. In general,
   the control of the over ground conditions during tunneling is achieved
   through two phases : the stability of the face of the excavation, and
   the improvement of the tunnel soil interface behind the shield. This
   paper presents surface and subsurface subsidence associated with
   shielded sewer tunnels of diameter 2.86 m constructed at the central
   zone of Alexandria city-Egypt. Surface and subsurface subsidence were
   monitored in the field using array of settlement points and
   inclinometers. Soil continuum,tunnel lining and the interface between
   them are idealized as an integrated system using non linear Finite
   element technique. The achieved results from the numerical model were
   justified with the field measurements obtained during the construction
   of the tunnel.},
