   alternative retail locations to questions of social equity and local
   economic development. The location of major urban developments such as
   shopping facilities has implications for resulting activity patterns.
   Car trips consume finite fossil resources and create polluting emissions
   of CO2 resulting in depletion of the ozone layer contributing to global
   warming. Decisions about the siting of major new shopping outlets
   therefore raise issues of environmental sustainability. (Parsons 1996;
   Anderson, Paolos, and Miller, 1996). This is recognised in the planning
   legislation in the UK in two key pieces of guidance to Planning
   Authorities, Planning Policy Guidance Notes 6 and 13 (DoE 1996; DoE
   1994).
   The retail sector has changed in two ways since the war. Economic
   concentration has meant that fewer large organisations have taken an
   increased market share (Bromley \& Thomas, 1993). The other major trend
   is the decentralisation of facilities (Schiller, 1988). This is
   particularly marked in food and groceries where the pattern has shifted
   from a large number of small independent traders grouped in shopping
   centres used on a daily basis to a dominance by much Fewer superstores
   located outside traditional centres used less frequently. Recent
   attention has been focused on the extent to which these trends have
   created groups of disadvantaged consumers (Westlake, 1993; the RTPI,
   1988).
   This paper explores the inter-relationship between these sets of issues
   in the contest of a case study of Pendle, a small borough in the north
   of England.},
