Year = {1997},
Volume = {1147},
Pages = {245-249},
Note = {III Pan-Pacific Conference on Brain Topography (BTOPPS III), TOKYO BAY,
   JAPAN, APR 01-04, 1997},
Abstract = {Background. Visual imagery is known to activate the early cortical
   stages of visual perception. We attempted to localize this activation
   more specifically.
   Methods. We employed the Fourier-based technique of Serene et al. to
   delineate the areas V1 and V2 in the visual cortex, and use this
   functional map as a basis to identify activation loci due to visual
   imagery for either mental calculations or induced clock images. For the
   latter case, we can compare activation due to perception and imagery.
   Results. For both tasks we find activation in visual areas V1 and V2
   across subjects. Activation strength during imagination appears to be
   related to the subjective task difficulty in both tasks. Furthermore
   (for clock images) we find a striking resemblance of activated areas
   during perception and imagination.
   Conclusions. We interpret these results as manifestations of backwards
   interaction to the earliest cortical stages of visual processing, areas
   V1 and V2 in the visual cortex during the generation of mental images.
   The similarity between activation for perception and imagination may
   indicate a topographic organization of visual imagery.},
