   PEOPLES R CHINA, MAY, 1998},
Organization = {US Nat Sci Fdn; Assoc Energy Engineers, US; Asea Brown Boveri Power
   Generat Ltd, Switzerland; EPFL, Ctr Rech \& Enseignement Technol Energet
   Integrees, Switzerland; China Machinery Ind Sloan Educ Dev Fund, China;
   Chinese Soc Power Engn, China; Univ Miami, Clean Energy Res Inst, US;
   Colorado State Univ, US; Zhejiang Univ, Inst Thermal Power Engn, China;
   Inst Francais Petrole, France; Univ Stuttgart, Inst Nucl Energy \&
   Energy Syst, Germany; Jiangsu Univ Sci \& Technol, China; Korea Inst
   Energy Res, Korea; Northeastern Univ, China; Ministry Chem Ind, Res Inst
   Chem Machinery, China; Shanghai Soc Energy Res, China; Shanghai Soc
   Environm Sci, China; Shanghai Inst Elect Power, China; Hong Kong
   Polytech Univ, Hong Kong; Tokyo Denki Univ, Japan; Zhengzhou Univ
   Technol, China},
Abstract = {Many surveys in recent years have shown that combustion equipment is
   often poorly operated and poorly maintained, and running at efficiencies
   that could be greatly improved if more attention were paid by operators
   and management.
   Tests were carried out in 1996-97 at six plants in the Shanghai area to
   monitor combustion conditions. These were conducted as part of a project
   supported by the UK Overseas Development Administration to assist the
   Shanghai Municipal Government to address problems of environmental
   degradation in the city. The project itself - known as the ``Support
   Programme for Urban Renewal of Shanghai (SPURS){''} was performed in
   close co-operation with the Shanghai Environmental Protection Bureau.
   Boilers and selected furnaces at six plants were tested regularly using
   portable combustion gas analysers. At one plant, firing oil and gas,
   combustion efficiencies were generally found to be good, although some
   improvements might be made. At all the other plants, where coal is
   fired, boilers were being operated at quite low levels of efficiency.
   Excessive amounts of coal are being consumed, and there are excessive
   releases of pollutants such as sulphur dioxide and of greenhouse gas
   such as carbon dioxide.},
